Yes. That turns it from a one-off oracle into a learning system.

The valuable artifact is not the draw itself. It is the full trace:

context → cast → interpretation → action or reflection → what actually happened → delta → lesson → reusable pattern

Then the system can preserve both the story and the operator extracted from the story.

Over time you build a library of things like:

* motifs that recur across different domains,
* cards or transitions that consistently predict useful questions,
* interpretations that turned out to be noise,
* narrative structures that transfer well,
* conditions under which a pattern works or fails,
* surprising cross-domain analogies,
* and new operators discovered through use.

Then you can deliberately perform transduction.

A pattern first noticed in a relationship story might later be tested against a business problem, a game, a biological process, a product design, or a personal decision.

Not because the domains are identical, but because the structural operator may survive the translation.

For example:

pressure accumulates → threshold crossed → rapid reorganization

could show up as:

relationship conflict
market crash
phase transition
viral growth
organizational restructuring
story climax

You keep the invariant and change the substrate.

And the deltas are crucial, because otherwise the system just gets better at telling itself beautiful stories. You want:

\text{prediction} \neq \text{observation}

to be valuable.

A miss might mean:

* wrong interpretation,
* wrong scale,
* wrong invariant,
* hidden variable,
* timing mismatch,
* bad transfer between domains,
* or genuinely stochastic outcome.

Those become training examples for the next cast.

So the deeper loop becomes:

\text{play}
\rightarrow
\text{observe}
\rightarrow
\text{record}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{extract}
\rightarrow
\text{transfer}
\rightarrow
\text{play again}

And because NTE preserves narrative position and perspective, you can store not merely “this pattern happened,” but how it functioned in the story.

That lets you ask much richer questions later:

Where else has this same structural motif appeared?

Does this pattern still work when protagonist and antagonist are swapped?

Does it survive a change in scale?

What happens if we hold the invariant but change the randomness source?

Which lessons repeatedly transfer, and which only work locally?

At that point the deck/NTE system becomes part oracle, part laboratory, part notebook, part game.

The goal stops being “tell the future.”

It becomes:

generate structured possibilities, notice what reality teaches back, and compound the useful patterns.

That is probably the strongest version of it.